-- title:
--   Poincaré bundle along the geometric Abel–Jacobi map of X₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Setting. Fix a prime $p$ and a non-zero natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$ (`hM`, `hpM`). Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with `hK` identifying $K$ with [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with an $L$-algebra structure making $L$ its fraction field, such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A$ (`hζA`); $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with the tower $A \to L \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be non-zero and such that its underlying Laurent series is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`).
--
--   The curve and its relative Picard datum. Write $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ for the morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), assumed proper, and let $\varepsilon$ be a section of it over $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Let $D$ be a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation for $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, that is, a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism `D.toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ together with a zero section; `hsm` and `hsep` assert that `D.toBase` is smooth and separated. The hypothesis `hrep` asserts that the type of data `RepresentsRelSubPic` for $X$, $\varepsilon$, the condition `algEquivZeroCut` and $D$ is non-empty; a witness is fixed as `hrep.some`. Such a datum consists of a rigidified line bundle `poincare` on $X \times_A D.P$ (rigidified along $\varepsilon$) whose restriction to every geometric fibre is algebraically equivalent to zero, which is universal among such rigidified line bundles on base changes of $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and whose pullback along the zero section is trivial.
--
--   The geometric model. The field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} = \mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb{Q}$ is an $A$-algebra and an $L$-algebra, compatibly. Let $M_\eta$ be a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (a proper, smooth relative dimension one integral scheme $M_\eta.C$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with a specified isomorphism of its function field with that field, a bijection between its closed points and the places, and the attendant compatibilities), and let $e_\eta : M_\eta.C \to X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be an isomorphism with $e_\eta$ followed by the second projection equal to $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$ (`heη`). Two hypotheses pin this identification: the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the open image of the finite chart [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) is non-empty as a scheme, and `hMηpin` requires that for every element $a$ of the $A$-subalgebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) of $K$, the element of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) obtained by transporting along $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ the germ at the generic point of that open of the pullback of $a$ has, as a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` requires that for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise, and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ (sections of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$), if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$, $e_\eta$ and the first projection, then the place attached to $x'$ by $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under the action of [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54). Finally `gpts` is a bijection between $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of `D.toBase`.
--
--   The data over $L$. The hypothesis `hDL` asserts that the base change of $D$ to $L$ represents, with the analogous condition `algEquivZeroCut`, the rigidified line bundles on the base change $X_L$ of $X$ with the base-changed section; `ajL` is a morphism $X_L \to (D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} L$, with `hajLε` stating that the base-changed section followed by `ajL` is the zero section of $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L$. The Abel–Jacobi property `hajL` requires that for every field $K'$, every morphism $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of $X_L$ over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by `ajL` has underlying module isomorphic to the line bundle (dual ideal module) of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` of $x$, tensored with the ideal module of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $t$ followed by the base-changed section. The hypothesis `hPL` asserts that the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` is isomorphic to the descent-to-$L$ (`BaseChange.ofR`) of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along the first projection of $D.P \times_A L$. A morphism $kL : X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to X \times_A L$ is given, compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, the second up to $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} L$).
--
--   The geometric Abel–Jacobi map. A morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}} : M_\eta.C \to D.P$ is given by `hajbar` as $e_\eta$ followed by $kL$, by `ajL` and by the first projection of $D.P \times_A L$, and `hajbar_over` states that $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ followed by `D.toBase` equals $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$. A $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M_\eta.C$ is given with `hεbar` (it induces the base change of $\varepsilon$ on $X$) and `hεbar_aj` (it is carried by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ to the base change of the zero section of $D$). The hypothesis `hpts_aj` requires that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M_\eta.C$ such that $s$ induces the base change of $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) equal to the difference of the one-point divisors at the places of $x$ and of $s$, with $\mathrm{gpts}$ of the class of $Dv$ equal to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   The point. Let $y$ be a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M_\eta.C$, let $\overline{y}$ be the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $X$ given by $y$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection (`hybar`), and let $a$ be the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of `D.toBase` given by $y$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ (`ha`).
--
--   Conclusion. The type of isomorphisms between the underlying module of the pullback along $a$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` and the tensor product of the line bundle (dual ideal module) of the relative effective Cartier divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint` of the point $\overline{y}$ with the ideal module of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the base-changed section $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ followed by $\varepsilon$ is non-empty; both are sheaves of modules on $X \times_A \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. In classical notation, $\mathcal{P}|_a \cong \mathcal{O}(\overline{y}) \otimes \mathcal{I}_{\varepsilon_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}}$.
--
--   This is the geometric-fibre form of the defining property of the Abel–Jacobi map for the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over the discrete valuation ring $A$: the restriction of the Poincaré bundle to the Abel–Jacobi image of a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point is the point bundle twisted by the ideal sheaf of the base section. It feeds the identification of $\mathrm{gpts}$ on classes of differences of points and the subsequent divisor-class computations on the Jacobian model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_XOneP_nonempty_poincare_pullbackAlong_iso_ofPoint_tensor_ofPoint_idealModule_of_eq_comp_ajbar_of_curveModel_twoChartModel_x1_mul.lean

